WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:05.220
A slow-motion ballet of shattering&nbsp;
spaceships, an army of arachnid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:10.260
warriors — and the surprising effect that&nbsp;
wasn't CGI at all. Here's what "Starship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:14.625
Troopers" looks like when you strip away the&nbsp;
special effects. Would you like to know more?

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:19.740
Some of the biggest, most thrilling scenes&nbsp;
in "Starship Troopers" saw the human heroes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:24.420
taking down the bugs. And one of the biggest&nbsp;
is a moment during a pitched battle between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:30.360
the two forces that sees the Mobile Infantry&nbsp;
under attack by a gigantic Tanker Bug. It's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:36.729
massive insect the size of an 18-wheeler, and it&nbsp;
seems nearly unstoppable. To finally put it down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.729 --> 00:00:40.140
Johnny Rico jumps off a ledge&nbsp;
and onto its back. Once there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:45.209
he blasts a small hole in its armor and tosses&nbsp;
in a grenade that blows it to smithereens.

00:00:45.394 --> 00:00:51.180
To film this dramatic sequence required a mix&nbsp;
of VFX both practical and computer-generated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:57.180
as well as some intense on-set stunt work. The&nbsp;
latter was achieved by having actor Casper Van&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:02.940
Dien jump from a real rock face, landing on a&nbsp;
green foam mat below. Later, with the use of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:08.580
a CGI stand-in and close-up inserts of Rico, the&nbsp;
entire sequence was assembled to make it look like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:13.892
the gung-ho former high school sports star was&nbsp;
using his fancy football moves to deadly effect.

00:01:13.892 --> 00:01:18.324
Other portions of the scene required different&nbsp;
kinds of special effects. A practical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.324 --> 00:01:22.380
full-scale version of the Tanker Bug's&nbsp;
back was created for Johnny to stand on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.524 --> 00:01:28.164
The rig sat atop a large apparatus capable of&nbsp;
moving the oversized bug prop back and forth.

00:01:28.164 --> 00:01:32.880
The shell was also recreated digitally later,&nbsp;
and the two shots were seamlessly joined to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:37.140
make it appear as though Rico was riding atop&nbsp;
a giant Tanker Bug, fighting for his life.

00:01:37.778 --> 00:01:42.060
The final part of Johnny Rico's takedown&nbsp;
of the Tanker Bug sees him being thrown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:45.240
from its back and hiding behind&nbsp;
the corpse of another Arachnid.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:49.560
When the dust of the explosion settles,&nbsp;
he stands up proudly in a hero moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:54.180
looking back at his troops — and the&nbsp;
audience — as the bug burns behind him.

00:01:54.334 --> 00:02:00.454
Creating just this one simple shot was nearly&nbsp;
as complicated as the entire sequence. First,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.454 --> 00:02:04.800
a background plate was shot of actor Casper&nbsp;
Van Dien, standing in front of a practical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:08.460
prop of a warrior bug carcass,&nbsp;
complete with background extras.

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:13.800
Then, the burning Tanker Bug was created&nbsp;
in CGI and composited behind him.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:18.780
Many of the big action set pieces in "Starship&nbsp;
Troopers" come on the Arachnid planet of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:23.700
Klendathu. The invasion of the planet winds up&nbsp;
being a botched mission and a total disaster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:28.920
for the Terran Federation. Filming Klendathu&nbsp;
meant more than just a lot of blue screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:34.380
green screen, and CGI — it also required a&nbsp;
full-scale set of a makeshift military outpost.

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:38.760
Behind the scenes videos from the making&nbsp;
of the film show the set while it was still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:43.620
under construction. Interestingly, a lot&nbsp;
of the seemingly futuristic elements of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:48.120
compound were actually made up of prefabricated&nbsp;
buildings from a company called Sprung Structures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:53.580
which offers high-performance, modular fabric&nbsp;
buildings, complete with furnishable interiors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:57.600
These allow various industries to set&nbsp;
up working spaces anywhere in the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:02.160
quickly and efficiently, and have been&nbsp;
used by companies like Tesla and FedEx,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:06.660
as well as for soundstages for TV&nbsp;
shows "Big Love" and "The Chosen."

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:09.840
Aside from the army of CGI bugs, in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:15.000
the entire set is practical — from the&nbsp;
buildings to the desert surroundings. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:19.380
behind-the-scenes video from Amalgamated&nbsp;
Dynamics also shows that the carcasses of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:24.900
the bugs — and people — strewn about the place&nbsp;
were meticulously sculpted and placed on the set.

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:29.460
In a sci-fi movie as ambitious as&nbsp;
"Starship Troopers," you'd probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:34.800
assume that most of the alien critters would be&nbsp;
computer-generated creations. And…you wouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:40.080
be wrong. A lot of them are. Vast armies of&nbsp;
CG Arachnids were created digitally to fill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:45.360
the scenes of attacking bugs. But the main&nbsp;
warrior bugs also had a working, motorized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:49.440
full-size robotic puppet counterpart&nbsp;
for certain scenes, including an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:54.334
early sequence that shows a bug lifting a&nbsp;
human soldier with its massive mandibles.

00:03:54.334 --> 00:03:59.160
The animatronic was designed and built by&nbsp;
VFX icon Phil Tippett, who had previously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:03.840
worked on "Jurassic Park," while a company&nbsp;
called Amalgamated Dynamics did the actual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:09.960
construction. And as Tippett told Cartoon Brew,&nbsp;
their usage had a very specific purpose, saying:

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:13.740
"Most of that centered around contact,&nbsp;
like when you actually see a real person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:17.520
about to be chopped in half and the&nbsp;
beak of a bug. […] You want to keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:22.320
the computer graphics people off-screen as much&nbsp;
as possible, because they don't look so good."

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:27.660
Having an actual, moving model certainly adds&nbsp;
a sense of realism to the warrior bug scenes..&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:32.820
What you don't see in the film, of course, are the&nbsp;
robot's many operators who control its movement.

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:34.680
"And, action."

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:37.587
"Up, up."

00:04:37.587 --> 00:04:42.180
Casper Van Dien isn't the only actor in&nbsp;
"Starship Troopers" to get to play with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:47.880
some big CG bugs. His co-star Neil Patrick&nbsp;
Harris — who played military intelligence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:53.760
officer and psychic Carl Jenkins — also&nbsp;
got in on the fun. The mix of human and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:57.660
CGI happens at the end of the movie&nbsp;
when Jenkins arrives in the aftermath&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.660 --> 00:05:03.300
of a major human victory. In a satirical&nbsp;
twist ending, Jenkins probes the mind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:08.609
the so-called "Brain Bug" and declares that&nbsp;
it's afraid while throngs of soldiers cheer.

00:05:08.609 --> 00:05:14.160
Although a life-size model of the Brain Bug&nbsp;
was used in earlier scenes, this one used a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:19.020
CGI creation, at least for the wide shots.&nbsp;
Seen in some behind-the-scenes footage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:23.520
the bug was portrayed on the set by a big&nbsp;
green stand-in partially covered in black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:28.200
fabric. This temporary, jury-rigged version&nbsp;
would be later removed in post-production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:34.255
and replaced with a CGI bug whose head&nbsp;
pulsates and whose body oozes slime.

00:05:34.401 --> 00:05:40.260
As its title implies, "Starship Troopers" is&nbsp;
about more than just soldiers — it also has a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:45.180
good number of starships. While there was plenty&nbsp;
of CGI employed to bring some of them to life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:50.400
many of the outer space sequences involving&nbsp;
ships — like Carmen's cruiser called the Rodger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:55.260
Young — were filmed using custom-built&nbsp;
models. One sequence involving dozens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.260 --> 00:06:00.300
of starships under attack used models created&nbsp;
by artisans at Sony's model shop Thunderstick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:07.412
with George Lucas' Industrial Light &amp; Magic, and&nbsp;
a VFX studio called Boss also lending a hand.

00:06:07.412 --> 00:06:12.480
Augmented with the use of some CG attack ships,&nbsp;
13 scale starships were created for the scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:19.260
and took a total of 14 months to assemble from&nbsp;
start to finish. The scene, which sees chaos erupt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:24.720
when the fleet meet an unexpected attack from the&nbsp;
bugs, required the VFX team to put together all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:31.140
of those ships, plus background elements. The CGI&nbsp;
added later required an advanced computer software&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.140 --> 00:06:36.735
program, though even that could barely handle&nbsp;
the task of compositing so many moving parts.

00:06:36.735 --> 00:06:41.220
Behind the scenes, the large models of the&nbsp;
starships were dangled in front of a black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:47.880
backdrop to capture just one of the more&nbsp;
than 200 individual elements. The large,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:51.720
empty warehouse space stands in&nbsp;
stark contrast to the final scene:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:56.534
a frame buzzing with starships,&nbsp;
explosions, and debris.

00:06:56.534 --> 00:07:01.680
During the incredible outer space action&nbsp;
scenes, a full-size set was built of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:06.720
interior of the Rodger Young's cockpit. But for&nbsp;
their forward view into space, which showed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:11.880
horrific destruction of the Terran fleet, special&nbsp;
digital effects were used, rather than any kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:17.492
of rear projection or on-set photography.&nbsp;
Instead, it was a standard green screen.

00:07:17.492 --> 00:07:20.700
It wasn't just the ships out the&nbsp;
window that were CGI — the window&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:25.701
itself was purely digital, as explained&nbsp;
by Sony Imageworks' Scott E. Anderson.

00:07:25.701 --> 00:07:29.241
"The flickering light on the windshield&nbsp;
is actually put in in digital."

00:07:29.241 --> 00:07:32.220
That allowed the animators to&nbsp;
add in subtle reflections of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:35.709
chaos happening outside the ship,&nbsp;
just like you'd see on real glass.

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:40.740
For director Paul Verhoeven, getting the&nbsp;
moving lights just right was like an orchestra,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.740 --> 00:07:45.000
and crucial to making the scene&nbsp;
convincing. Colored lamps were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:48.780
moved slowly from side to side to cast&nbsp;
the right lighting on the physical set,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.780 --> 00:07:53.640
and were replaced later by scenes of massive&nbsp;
burning ships and explosive collisions.

00:07:53.801 --> 00:07:56.261
Amid the space battle above the bug planets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.261 --> 00:08:01.380
it's not just the Rodger Young that's taking&nbsp;
fire. Multiple massive starships are struck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:06.480
splitting open and jettisoning crew&nbsp;
into open space. Fiery debris from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:11.280
entire fleet rains down as shuttles escape their&nbsp;
motherships and try to stay alive in the frenzy.

00:08:11.534 --> 00:08:15.725
At least one of those wrecked vessels was&nbsp;
created with elaborate model work. 

00:08:15.725 --> 00:08:20.160
It's&nbsp;just one of many such ships built for&nbsp;
the scene, and it's only when you see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:24.900
the whole model that you understand just&nbsp;
how large the thing really is. Ultimately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:29.460
the ship measured in at a whopping&nbsp;
18 feet and was built with removable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:33.120
sections to make the destruction scenes&nbsp;
easier to shoot in stop motion later.

00:08:33.267 --> 00:08:37.527
"We also had two stop motion animators&nbsp;
who literally frame by frame,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.527 --> 00:08:41.400
as the ship pulled apart, bent&nbsp;
the middle pieces of the decks."

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:46.140
It's mind boggling how much work went&nbsp;
into each model, with astonishing levels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:51.480
of detail to convince audiences that this was&nbsp;
a massive starship cut in two by the enemy.

00:08:51.480 --> 00:08:55.860
The Rodger Young isn't the only&nbsp;
starship piloted by Carmen. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.860 --> 00:08:59.880
that ship is destroyed she also gets&nbsp;
behind the controls of an escape shuttle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:03.360
a sequence which required a&nbsp;
couple different moments of CGI.

00:09:03.751 --> 00:09:08.851
When Carmen and Zander reach the shuttle&nbsp;
bay, the pod they actually get into is real,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.851 --> 00:09:11.470
while the rest of the pods&nbsp;
were added in post production.

00:09:11.470 --> 00:09:14.460
"We only had the first part of the&nbsp;
set, and the second part of the set,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.460 --> 00:09:17.229
further away in the background, was added later."

00:09:17.229 --> 00:09:22.080
As with the larger starship, a green screen is&nbsp;
also used to make room for computer-generated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:26.580
images of outer space beyond the windows of&nbsp;
the shuttle. Footage from filming the scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:30.060
shows actress Denise Richards sitting&nbsp;
in the cockpit with her headgear on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:33.000
and a green screen out the&nbsp;
window of the small vessel.

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:36.960
As described by Paul Verhoeven, the&nbsp;
crash and destruction of the Rodger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:41.628
Young was crafted in CGI and placed&nbsp;
in the background of this very shot.

00:09:41.731 --> 00:09:44.340
"The foreground was shot on a gimble,&nbsp;
because that's where you get all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:48.540
trembling and the strange, uh,&nbsp;
the positions of this thing."

00:09:48.540 --> 00:09:51.660
These active movements help&nbsp;
give the scene a rocky,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.660 --> 00:09:56.034
unsteady look as Carmen pilots the&nbsp;
shuttle away from the destruction.

00:09:56.034 --> 00:10:00.600
There are some tense moments in the finale&nbsp;
of "Starship Troopers," and one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:05.400
nail-biting is when Carmen is captured by the&nbsp;
bugs. Finding herself trapped underground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:09.660
where she's due to become food for them,&nbsp;
she fails to save her new love, Zander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:14.640
who has his head stabbed and emptied&nbsp;
out by the Brain Bug. She is, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:19.020
able to save herself. But if you were on&nbsp;
the set of the film the day that scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.020 --> 00:10:23.468
was shot you would have seen something very&nbsp;
different than what ended up on the screen.

00:10:23.468 --> 00:10:27.600
Showing up in more behind-the-scenes footage&nbsp;
is the moment where Carmen faces off against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:31.800
one of the bugs. But for the close-up&nbsp;
shots of her face, she was actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:36.448
staring down director Paul Verhoeven while&nbsp;
he shouted and held a broom over his head.

00:10:36.448 --> 00:10:38.138
"Da da da da da ah!"

00:10:38.138 --> 00:10:41.400
For shots involving the full&nbsp;
bug, he'd be substituted for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:46.109
the full-size puppet of the Brain Bug,&nbsp;
or a CGI model, depending on the shot.

00:10:46.201 --> 00:10:51.361
Meanwhile, Zander's death was pretty much&nbsp;
filmed as it ultimately appeared onscreen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:56.040
Production built several models of&nbsp;
actor Patrick Muldoon's head to sub&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.040 --> 00:11:00.000
in for progressively horrifying shots&nbsp;
as the Brain Bug sucked out his brains.

00:11:00.267 --> 00:11:02.880
All of this was done in a sprawling indoor set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:06.269
of subterranean insect caves&nbsp;
that was built for the film.

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:10.980
All these practical onscreen elements just&nbsp;
prove how much work went into bringing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.980 --> 00:11:14.940
Starship Troopers to life, and are part&nbsp;
of the reason the film holds up so well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.940 --> 00:11:20.460
after all these years. Using CGI with a light&nbsp;
touch to augment the practical effects rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.460 --> 00:11:25.020
than always replace them outright means&nbsp;
that every piece of the action feels all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:29.829
the more real to the audience. Yeah,&nbsp;
even a giant oozing brain-shaped bug.

00:11:29.829 --> 00:11:33.429
"And then strike. Ooh!"

